---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../lib/guides.ts';
const guide = guideBySlug('base64-utf8-base64url-file-decoder');
const greeting = 'Hello, café ☕';
const encoded = 'SGVsbG8sIGNhZsOpIOKYlQ==';
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} keywords="Base64 UTF-8 encode decode, Base64URL decoder, Base64 file encoder, Base64 padding, decode JWT Base64URL, data URI Base64">
  <p>Base64 is simple only when the bytes are ASCII. The moment a payload contains an emoji, a binary file, a JWT segment, or compressed data, a copy-pasted browser snippet can give you the wrong answer. The fix is to identify the bytes and the Base64 variant before you decode.</p>

  <h2>Try a UTF-8 Base64 round trip</h2>
  <p>Paste <code>{greeting}</code> into the <a href="/base64-encoder-decoder/">Base64 encoder and decoder</a> and choose <strong>Encode UTF-8 → Base64</strong>. The result is <code>{encoded}</code>. Decode that value to recover the exact text, including the accented character and emoji.</p>
  <p>A legacy <code>btoa('café')</code> call throws because it accepts a byte-oriented string, not arbitrary Unicode text. A safe implementation encodes a JavaScript string to UTF-8 bytes first, then encodes those bytes as Base64.</p>

  <h2>Base64URL is not a different payload format</h2>
  <p>Base64URL carries the same bytes as Base64. It substitutes <code>-</code> for <code>+</code> and <code>_</code> for <code>/</code>, which avoids URL escaping. It also commonly removes trailing <code>=</code> padding. JWT header and payload sections are Base64URL; they are not encrypted merely because they are compact.</p>
  <pre><code>{`standard Base64: eyJzdWIiOiIxMjMifQ==
Base64URL:        eyJzdWIiOiIxMjMifQ`}</code></pre>
  <p>Use the tool’s <strong>Base64URL ↔ Base64</strong> action to normalize either form. To inspect a complete token, use the <a href="/jwt-decoder/">JWT decoder</a>; it separates the header, claims, and signature rather than treating the whole token as one Base64 string.</p>

  <h2>Encode a file as bytes, not as text</h2>
  <p>Images, certificates, compressed payloads, and other files need a binary read. Choose <strong>Encode local file</strong>; the page reads the bytes on your device and produces Base64. Do not decode those bytes into text unless you know their character encoding. A binary-looking result is often correct.</p>
  <p>A data URI has a prefix such as <code>data:image/png;base64,</code>. The decoder accepts that form and removes the prefix before decoding the payload. For image workflows, continue to the <a href="/image-base64/">image-to-Base64 converter</a>.</p>

  <h2>Recognize compressed Base64</h2>
  <p>Many GZip byte streams start with hexadecimal <code>1f 8b 08</code>, commonly rendered as <code>H4sI</code> in Base64. Base64 decoding succeeds but produces compressed bytes—not readable text. Send that value to the <a href="/gzip-base64/">GZip Base64 decoder</a>, which performs the second decompression step.</p>

  <h2>Share a synthetic reproducer</h2>
  <p>A shared link can preserve the input and selected conversion operation. That is helpful for a small synthetic example in a ticket or technical question, but it is not a secret store: anybody with the link can read its included input. Replace real API tokens, customer values, and private file contents before sharing.</p>

